Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix(sim): _sim_cox array-domain spatial support -- the
 no-covariate branch now takes support and areas from the constructor's
 integration arrays via an INDEXED lookup by comp_grid_id (never a geometric
 self-join, never silent reliance on comp_grid_id == row position). DEFECT,
 quantified: for array domains self.A IS the comp grid, so
 comp_grid.sjoin(self.A) returned cell-neighbor pairs. Exact geometry predicts
 5329 rows (degrees 4/6/9 corner/edge/interior, mean 8.53); the
 float-constructed grid actually produced 4761 rows, degree histogram {4:36,
 6:228, 9:361} -- roughly 11 percent of adjacencies fail at the ulp level
 (cell edges built as j*w + w vs (j+1)*w), so the sampling weights were
 FLOAT-NOISE-DEPENDENT. Under a flat field the Poisson mean was inflated 7.62x
 (4761/625) and a full-degree interior cell carried 2.25x (= 9/4) the weight
 of a corner cell. GeoDataFrame domains were unaffected (sjoin against a
 genuine polygon, deduplicated); verified byte-identical fixed-seed gdf
 simulation across this fix. New test_sim_cox_array_domain_support_regression
 (RED against the old code: 'cell support has 4761 rows, expected 625'; GREEN
 after) pins the mathematical targets under a zero field: support exactly
 n_xy^2 unique cells, uniform cell probabilities, Poisson mean = T_internal.
 test_real_sim_cox_uses_exact_integral EDITED from mirroring the duplicated
 construction to asserting the invariant lam == Itot_time * Itot_xy (traced);
 test-edit sign-off: Terhi, alongside the regression test. Verified: 36/36;
 inference value+gradient pins bit-identical.

